# tools/cr/crypto_verify_witness.rish — constant-time byte-string equality (Monocypher's crypto_verify16/32/64) authored in pure Rye: Season G's
# constant-time comparison primitive, the piece the two HMAC modules each named as an open horizon, proven correct against Zig's independent
# std.crypto.timing_safe.eql across every single-bit difference at all three Monocypher widths (Season G · Cryptography).
#
# Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season G thread (Cryptography · Rye-native, Monocypher-parity, audit-ready).
# Design read: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-175524_rye-first-crypto-parity-and-the-decision-wave.md — Season G's crux rungs name the primitive,
# the parity witness, and "a constant-time discipline note (timing-safety as a named horizon)." This primitive is the one corner of that
# horizon correct by STRUCTURE rather than measurement: its control flow visits every byte regardless of contents, so no branch depends on the
# compared data. crypto/CONSTANT_TIME.md is its natural home in the auditor's reading.
# Kin: crypto/poly1305.rye (whose tag every AEAD open checks) · crypto/hmac_sha512.rye · crypto/hmac_sha256.rye (which named this horizon:
# "verifying a MAC wants a constant-time compare, which the composer that uses this primitive supplies") · crypto/aead.rye · crypto/xchacha20.rye.
# Monocypher (vendor/monocypher, CC0/BSD-dual) is the parity TARGET, never a copied line — our own arithmetic, studied clean-room from the
# public API and Zig's own std.crypto.timing_safe (.claude/rules/gratitude-licenses.md).
#
# crypto/verify.rye authors the primitive from the idea, not from a copied line: two byte strings are equal iff the OR of their byte-wise XORs
# is zero; the fold `diff |= a[i] ^ b[i]` visits every byte with no early exit, so the loop count depends on the public length and never on the
# possibly-secret contents, and the final zero-test `(x | -x) >> 7` reads any difference as one branch-free flag. The primitive calls no
# std.crypto. Correctness is proven at selftest against Zig's independent std.crypto.timing_safe.eql: at all three Monocypher widths (16, 32,
# 64) an equal span compares equal and flipping EVERY single bit of EVERY byte compares unequal, our answer matching Zig's in every case; the
# fixed-width doors agree with the generic engine, and the edge refuses a length mismatch and an over-long compare before a byte is folded.
# Constant-time timing-safety stays a named horizon — the byte loop is data-independent by construction, yet a timing CLAIM wants measurement,
# so correctness is proven here rather than a timing claim boasted. Purely local — no key, no signature, no network, no funds, no real device.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/cr/crypto_verify_witness.rish

say "crypto-verify: Language EN — Season G's constant-time comparison primitive: Monocypher's crypto_verify16/32/64, the piece hmac_sha512.rye and hmac_sha256.rye each named as an open horizon ('verifying a MAC wants a constant-time compare, which the composer supplies'). Every MAC, tag, or signature-equality check needs a comparison whose running time does not leak WHERE two strings first differ — a naive left-to-right compare that returns on the first mismatch lets an attacker forge a tag one timed byte at a time. This module removes that leak by construction."
say "crypto-verify: Style Radiant — the law is stated positively: two byte strings are equal iff the OR of their byte-wise XORs is zero. The fold visits every byte with no early exit, so the loop count depends on the public length and never on the possibly-secret contents; the final zero-test reads any difference — high bit or low — as one branch-free flag. The same key and strings always yield the same equal / not-equal answer."
say "crypto-verify: Lens TAME — the primitive calls no std.crypto; it is authored from the idea, bounded input (max_compare_bytes), contract asserts on every function, a length-mismatch and an over-long compare refused at the edge before any byte is folded. Correctness holds against Zig's independent std.crypto.timing_safe.eql: at all three Monocypher widths an equal span compares equal and flipping EVERY single bit of EVERY byte compares unequal, our answer matching Zig's in every case. Monocypher-source parity is a companion witness. Purely local — no key, no signature, no network, no funds, no device; the byte loop is data-independent by construction, and constant-time timing-safety stays the Season's named horizon, correctness proven here rather than a timing claim boasted."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let bin = "crypto/bin/verify"

let build = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p crypto/bin && env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build crypto/verify.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "crypto-verify: build failed — ${build.err}"

let outfile = "crypto/bin/verify.out"
let selftest = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} selftest > ${outfile} 2>&1"]
assert selftest.ok else "crypto-verify: selftest exited non-zero — see ${outfile}"

let shown = run ["sh" "-c" "cat ${outfile}"]
say "crypto-verify: ${shown.out}"

let green = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -q 'GREEN crypto-verify' ${outfile} && echo yes"]
assert (trim green.out) == "yes" else "crypto-verify: selftest did not print the GREEN crypto-verify line"
